1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:03,300
Right now I’m inside the elevator of a medical building.

2
00:00:03,367 --> 00:00:06,500
This is already my third ride in this elevator today.

3
00:00:06,567 --> 00:00:08,033
Wasted trips so far: two.

4
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:10,533
"That’s why I said we should’ve gone to the university hospital from the start."

5
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:12,600
That was wrong too.

6
00:00:12,667 --> 00:00:14,233
Let’s rewind thirty minutes.

7
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:21,067
Saturday morning, my kid fell down at the park. They landed on their wrist and scraped a knee.

8
00:00:21,133 --> 00:00:27,233
Once the crying stopped, I took them to the medical building in our neighborhood. There was a sign on every floor.

9
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:29,767
Looking up at the signs one by one, my kid asked.

10
00:00:29,833 --> 00:00:35,400
"Dad, remember what I asked last time? Internal medicine fixes the inside, and surgery fixes the outside?"

11
00:00:35,467 --> 00:00:40,133
"So where do I go? My knee is outside, but my wrist hurts on the inside."

12
00:00:40,700 --> 00:00:45,133
"Hmm… the knee is outside, so surgery. But there’s no surgery clinic in this building?"

13
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:48,767
"Then the wrist that hurts inside is more urgent, so internal medicine! Fifth floor!"

14
00:00:48,833 --> 00:00:51,500
Quite logical. To my ears, anyway.

15
00:00:52,067 --> 00:00:58,433
Fifth floor, internal medicine reception. The receptionist looked at my kid’s wrist and pointed at the floor.

16
00:00:58,500 --> 00:01:02,633
"Sir, bones and joints go to orthopedics. Third floor."

17
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:04,233
Wasted trip: one.

18
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:07,767
In the elevator, Mom sighed.

19
00:01:07,833 --> 00:01:13,367
"That’s why I said university hospital from the start. A big place sees everything at once."

20
00:01:13,933 --> 00:01:18,400
Third floor, orthopedics. After an X-ray, the doctor pointed at the screen.

21
00:01:18,467 --> 00:01:21,133
"The bone is fine. Just a slight sprain."

22
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:30,333
"Ma’am, a neighborhood clinic is plenty for this. And if you go to a university hospital without a referral letter, health insurance won’t cover it and you pay the whole bill."

23
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:32,000
Mom: one, too.

24
00:01:32,567 --> 00:01:34,200
"Then what does surgery do?"

25
00:01:34,267 --> 00:01:35,467
"It fixes things by hand."

26
00:01:36,033 --> 00:01:41,033
While we waited for the bandage, I stood behind the skeleton model in the corner of the waiting room and turned on my phone.

27
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:45,400
The creature called Dad, when cornered, will search even from behind a skeleton.

28
00:01:45,467 --> 00:01:51,833
To be fair, it’s confusing. There are twenty-six specialties, and five of them have “surgery” in the name.

29
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:57,067
On top of that, we call both the neighborhood clinic and the university hospital just “the hospital.”

30
00:01:57,133 --> 00:01:59,433
So today we look at just two things.

31
00:01:59,500 --> 00:02:02,400
What it’s fixed with. And where to go first.

32
00:02:02,967 --> 00:02:04,967
"The friend with the hurt wrist, come on in!"

33
00:02:05,033 --> 00:02:06,200
Yes, coming.

34
00:02:06,767 --> 00:02:08,767
First, internal medicine and surgery.

35
00:02:08,833 --> 00:02:12,900
The line isn’t inside versus outside. It’s what you fix it with.

36
00:02:12,967 --> 00:02:17,067
Internal medicine mostly uses medicine; surgery mostly uses hands. In other words, operations.

37
00:02:17,133 --> 00:02:22,300
Surgery comes from a Greek word that means “work done by hand.”

38
00:02:22,367 --> 00:02:27,233
That’s why appendicitis, which happens inside the belly, is operated on by surgeons. Even though it’s inside the body.

39
00:02:27,800 --> 00:02:32,733
Here’s a fun part: in old Europe, surgeons belonged to the same guild as barbers.

40
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:41,133
London, 1540. The people who cut hair and the people who stitched wounds shared one hall. Both were good with blades.

41
00:02:41,200 --> 00:02:48,367
Then in 1745, a law split them up. Surgeons with surgeons, barbers with barbers.

42
00:02:48,433 --> 00:02:55,433
Some say the red on a barber pole is blood and the white is bandages, but that’s not established fact.

43
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:01,667
In the East too, surgery originally dealt mostly with illnesses that showed on the outside, like boils and wounds.

44
00:03:01,733 --> 00:03:08,200
So my kid was half right, a long time ago. Now the hands just reach inside the body too.

45
00:03:08,767 --> 00:03:11,067
From those two trunks, branches split off.

46
00:03:11,133 --> 00:03:21,400
From surgery: orthopedics for bones and joints, neurosurgery for the brain and nerves, thoracic surgery for the heart and lungs, plastic surgery for restoring how things look.

47
00:03:21,467 --> 00:03:26,200
Nose, ears and throat: ENT. Eyes: ophthalmology. Skin: dermatology.

48
00:03:26,267 --> 00:03:29,900
And pediatrics, which looks at a child’s body at a child’s size.

49
00:03:29,967 --> 00:03:42,233
So, last time’s question: where does a cold go? Internal medicine. For a child, pediatrics. And if the nose or ears hurt a lot, ENT sees colds too. Just pick by what bothers you most.

50
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:44,967
I also learned how to read clinic signs.

51
00:03:45,033 --> 00:03:50,933
If the specialty is part of the clinic’s name, like “So-and-so Dermatology Clinic,” a specialist in that field runs it.

52
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:59,467
If it says “So-and-so Clinic” with a small “treats: dermatology” next to it, they do see those patients, but the doctor may not be a specialist in that field.

53
00:04:00,033 --> 00:04:02,233
Now Mom’s turn: the size of hospitals.

54
00:04:02,300 --> 00:04:09,633
The bottom step is the neighborhood clinic. Above it, a hospital with thirty or more beds, then a general hospital with a hundred or more beds and many departments.

55
00:04:09,700 --> 00:04:16,633
At the top: tertiary hospitals, designated by the state for difficult, serious illnesses. There are only forty-seven nationwide.

56
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:23,800
What you need to go up a step is a referral letter. Your neighborhood doctor writes it when they think a bigger place should see you.

57
00:04:23,867 --> 00:04:30,167
Without it you can still be seen, but insurance won’t cover it and you pay everything. Emergencies are the exception.

58
00:04:30,233 --> 00:04:38,333
Why set it up that way? The top step has few seats. If we line up there with a sprained wrist, someone who is seriously ill has to wait that much longer.

59
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:44,733
Emergency rooms are the same. An ER isn’t where you get seen fast. It’s where the most urgent are seen first.

60
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:47,800
They line people up again, not by arrival, but by urgency.

61
00:04:47,867 --> 00:04:55,000
So since autumn 2024, if you go to a big hospital’s ER with mild symptoms, you pay ninety percent of the bill yourself.

62
00:04:55,067 --> 00:05:00,267
If a child gets a fever at night or on a weekend, there are Moonlight Children’s Hospitals that see kids at night and on holidays.

63
00:05:00,333 --> 00:05:05,667
If you don’t know where to go, you can call 119 and ask. They’ll tell you which hospitals are open.

64
00:05:05,733 --> 00:05:12,133
But if someone can’t breathe well, isn’t fully conscious, or is bleeding a lot, don’t think twice. Call 119 right away.

65
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:14,167
So, to sum up:

66
00:05:14,233 --> 00:05:17,000
Internal medicine with medicine, surgery with hands.

67
00:05:17,067 --> 00:05:20,267
Start in the neighborhood; go to the big hospital with a letter.

68
00:05:20,333 --> 00:05:24,533
Choose the specialty by where it hurts, and the size of the hospital by how much it hurts.

69
00:05:25,967 --> 00:05:32,233
With the wrist bandaged, we came down to the first floor. In front of the lobby directory, I crouched down to my kid’s eye level.

70
00:05:32,300 --> 00:05:37,600
"Earlier I said surgery is the outside and internal medicine is the inside, right? That was wrong."

71
00:05:37,667 --> 00:05:42,167
"Internal medicine doctors fix things with medicine, and surgeons fix things directly with their hands."

72
00:05:42,233 --> 00:05:51,300
"And each part of the body has doctors who know it best. Bones: orthopedics. Nose and ears: ENT. A growing body like yours: pediatrics."

73
00:05:51,367 --> 00:05:56,267
"If you’re not sure what’s wrong, first go to a neighborhood doctor who knows us well."

74
00:05:56,333 --> 00:06:00,000
"If that doctor thinks you need a bigger hospital, they write you a letter."

75
00:06:00,567 --> 00:06:01,433
"A letter?"

76
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:06,967
"Yes. A referral letter. A letter to the next doctor that says, “Please take care of this child.”"

77
00:06:07,033 --> 00:06:11,533
"But if it’s hard to breathe or you’re badly hurt, skip the letter and call 119 right away."

78
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:16,433
My kid looked up at the directory and pointed at it one floor at a time.

79
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:20,500
"Bones, third floor. Nose, fourth floor. Me, second floor."

80
00:06:20,567 --> 00:06:23,833
Then they held out the bandaged wrist toward me.

81
00:06:23,900 --> 00:06:26,133
"The third-floor doctor fixed me today."

82
00:06:26,700 --> 00:06:32,333
Surgery fixes the outside. Bigger hospitals are always better. We weren’t the only couple who thought so, right?

83
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:35,633
As we left the building, my kid stopped walking.

84
00:06:35,700 --> 00:06:38,133
"Dad, where’s my bike?"

85
00:06:38,700 --> 00:06:42,133
The bike we rode out on that morning was nowhere to be seen.

86
00:06:42,200 --> 00:06:44,433
"…It must’ve been stolen. Let’s go to the police box."

87
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:46,900
"If they catch the thief, do they go to jail? Right away?"

88
00:06:46,967 --> 00:06:48,767
The next episode is about this question.
